{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#519","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#519","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#519\",\"parent_revision\":\"d4391d28e2c1ffbd923a98c614e1cb44ef33604b\",\"root_revision\":\"1f3b98e3d7956260652dab9c341cbcfa43d18189b9454f6568313e7ab7b2c973\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe repo has shared GitHub API interaction scattered across multiple scripts and plugins (backlog.py, daily-releases scripts, etc.), each with its own inline GitHub client construction, SSL handling, and token management. Success: a single shared github_utils library installable by any script or plugin in the repo via PEP 723 [tool.uv.sources] or a proper package dependency, so all GitHub API work goes through one place. Done when all existing inline GitHub client code is replaced with imports from the shared library and CI stays green.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAssessment date: 2026-04-23\\n\\n| # | Condition | Status | Source |\\n|---|-----------|--------|--------|\\n| 1 | Identified scripts with inline GitHub clients | AVAILABLE | Item description: backlog.py, daily-releases scripts |\\n| 2 | Shared library location strategy | MISSING | PEP 723 sources vs workspace package \u2014 architecture decision |\\n| 3 | `backlog_core/github.py` already as reference | AVAILABLE | Established GitHub client in development-harness |\\n\\nVerdict: READY with one architecture decision (package distribution mechanism). Likely use workspace package approach since backlog_core already exists.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **consolidate repo-wide github api utilities into a shared library** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-23)\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\nP2 Refactor. Medium complexity. Cross-cutting change across scripts and plugins. Zero behavioral change.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- A `github_utils` package exists as a proper importable library within the repo (PEP 723 [tool.uv.sources] or as a workspace package)\\n- All existing inline GitHub client code in identified scripts replaced with imports from `github_utils`\\n- `github_utils` handles: token retrieval from env, SSL configuration, client instantiation, and common error handling\\n- CI remains green after migration\\n- At minimum, `backlog.py` and at least one other identified script migrated\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github.py` \u2014 refactor to extract shared utilities\\n- `scripts/github_utils.py` or new workspace package \u2014 shared utilities\\n- `scripts/backlog.py` and other identified scripts \u2014 migrate to shared library\\n\\n### Story\\n\\nAs a developer writing a script or plugin that calls the GitHub API, I need a single shared `github_utils` library importable by any script in the repo so I don't repeat inline GitHub client construction, SSL handling, and token management across multiple files.\\n\",\"digest\":\"7dee2315376fc3d2f4e9e056ee5430d9218b10a7267b5d5cb03a524e0246c4c4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg0QPw\"}"}